      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally, Shageluk was a traditional village of the Deg Hit'an Athabascan people.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kupi.com/en/explore/united-states/shageluk/history",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Shageluk’s growth has always hinged on its position along the Innoko River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.kupi.com/en/explore/united-states/shageluk/history",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the early 1900s, epidemics and outmigration shrank Shageluk, yet Russian missions, gold mining, and a 1918 reindeer program left lasting marks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/shageluk_alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Shageluk moved two miles south in 1966 to escape flooding and rebuilt on higher ground.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shageluk%2C_Alaska",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
